the russian president vladimir putin  said again today that he does not want a  war with europe despite describing the  situation in ukraine's donbass region as  genocide it is a false claim it is a  heavily loaded claim and it is a claim  that could be used as a pretext for  invasion the donbass region which we've  highlighted on that map is in eastern  ukraine it has been the scene of heavy  fighting between ukrainian forces and  russian back troops since 2014 and it is  true to say that thousands have died and  millions have fled for safety some of  those into russia  in those intervening years president  putin has granted passports to more than  600 000 people living in the region  thereby effectively making them russian  nationals and so he could in theory send  troops in on the pretense of protecting  those new russian nationals against  these false claims of genocide  so with all that in mind let's have a  look at russian troop movements and what  they might or might not tell us about  the coming days and the possibility of  an all-out invasion of ukraine  now first this video which  caused a bit of a stir around the world  when it was released by the russian  defense ministry it shows tanks from the  southern and western military districts  returning to their bases on the face of  it a good sign but western officials  that i've spoken to give it very little  credibility they point out that there is  a lot of movement backwards and forwards  but most of it in one direction and that  is towards the ukrainian border  so we're now going to show you a series  of satellite photos to explain why they  might conclude that  now here we can see in the first  satellite image the garrison at yelner  in western russia  large amounts of weaponry were moved  there last year including 700 tanks and  ballistic missile launchers but take a  look at this image that was taken on  sunday  and you'll see that has been a lot of  movement and more units have arrived the  next image is an overview of the celotti  garrison taken on january the 12th just  northeast of the ukrainian city kharkiv  now i want you to concentrate on the top  right hand corner of the image because  in the next image taken within the last  48 hours you can quite clearly see  there has been increased activity  and as a final example this time we're  looking at the build-up of helicopters  at a base in crimea 67 helicopters in  total some of them troop carriers some  of them ground attack aircraft any of  them  or all of them could be used as part of  an invasion now that's just a sample of  the satellite imagery and video of  russian troop movements but it does  i think paint a picture and explain why  nato and western officials  remain so concerned
 

